Plaintiffs are the copyright owners of certain copyrighted sound recordings ("Copyrighted Recordings"). As copyright owners, their rights include the exclusive rights to reproduce and distribute the Copyrighted Recordings to the public. (Id. ¶¶ 10-11). They allege Blumenreich has, without permission, used an online media distribution system to download at least sixteen different Copyrighted Recordings, distribute them to the public, and/or make them available for distribution to others. Plaintiffs seek statutory damages for copyright infringement pursuant to 17 U.S.C. § 504(c). They further assert they are entitled to attorneys' fees and costs under 17 U.S.C. § 505. Finally, Plaintiffs request injunctive relief, enjoining Blumenreich from further reproduction or distribution of any Copyrighted Recordings and additionally requiring the destruction of any such recordings in his possession, custody or control. (Id. ¶¶ 15-16).
Accordingly, the Court must evaluate Plaintiffs' claims for relief. Plaintiffs' motion for final default judgment seeks relief based on three provisions of the Copyright Act. Specifically, Plaintiffs seek statutory damages under 17 U.S.C. § 504, an injunction pursuant   to 17 U.S.C. § 502 and costs under 17 U.S.C. § 505.
Section 504 imposes liability against an infringer of copyright for either actual or statutory damages. 17 U.S.C. § 504(a). The statute permits recovery of not less than $750 or more than $30,000 per work infringed. Id. § 504(c). Plaintiffs must satisfy two requirements to present a prima facie case of direct infringement: (1) they must show ownership of the allegedly infringed material; and (2) they must demonstrate the alleged infringers violate at least one exclusive right granted to copyright holders under 17 U.S.C. § 106. See id. § 501(a) (infringement occurs when alleged infringer engages in activity listed in section 106). Activities listed in section 106 include reproduction of copyrighted work in copies, as well as distribution of copies of the copyrighted work to the public by sale or other transfer of ownership, or by rental, lease, or lending. Id. § 106(1) & (3).
Plaintiffs allege Defendant Blumenreich has, without permission, used an online media distribution system to download music recordings in which they own a copyright, distribute them to the public, and/or make them available for distribution to others. (Compl. ¶ 12). By virtue of the sanction imposed striking his answer and consequent default, Defendant Blumenreich has admitted to the   truth of this allegation. Therefore, the Court finds Plaintiffs have established a cause of action under § 504(a). See In re Aimster Copyright Litig., 334 F.3d 643, 645 (7th Cir. 2003) (swapping of computer files containing copyrighted music, which involves making and transmitting a digital copy of the music, infringes copyright, with swappers being the direct infringers); A&MRecords, Inc. v. Napster, Inc., 239 F.3d 1004, 1014 (9th Cir. 2001) (users of online file sharing system who upload file names to the search index for others to copy violate copyright holders' distribution rights, users who download files containing copyrighted music violate copyright holders' reproduction rights).
Having concluded Plaintiffs are entitled to default judgment on their § 504(a) claim, the Court turns to the question of relief. As a general rule, courts may enter a default judgment awarding damages without a hearing only if the amount of damages is a liquidated sum, an amount capable of mathematical calculation, or an amount demonstrated by detailed affidavits. James v. Frame, 6 F.3d 307, 310 (5th Cir. 1993); United Artists Corp. v. Freeman, 605 F.2d 854, 857 (5th Cir. 1979). It is a matter within the court's discretion whether to hold a hearing before awarding damages in a default judgment. See Fed. R. Civ. P. 55(b)(2) (in determining amount of damages entered in default, court may conduct such hearings or order such references to determine amount of damages "as it deems necessary   and proper"); James, 6 F.3d at 310 (district court has wide latitude in deciding whether to require evidentiary hearing before entering default judgment). Here, Plaintiffs have not requested a hearing and have moved only for statutory, and not actual, damages. Accordingly, the Court finds a hearing is not necessary to calculate damages.
Section 504(c) states, in pertinent part:

the copyright owner may elect, at any time before final judgment is rendered, to recover, instead of actual damages and profits, an award of statutory damages for all infringements involved in the action, with respect to any one work, for which any one infringer is liable individually, or for which any two or more infringers are liable jointly and severally, in a sum of not less than $750 or more than $30,000 as the court considers just. 17 U.S.C. § 504(c)(1). Plaintiffs alleges Defendant downloaded, reproduced and/or distributed sixteen separate Copyrighted Recordings. (Compl. ¶ 10, Exs. A & B). They seek only the minimum damages authorized by statute for each infringement. Accordingly, Plaintiffs are entitled to $12,000 in statutory damages. See Broadcast Music, Inc. v. Xanthas, Inc., 855 F.2d 233, 237 (5th Cir. 1988) (under § 504(c) trial court must award damages according to number of separate works infringed).
Plaintiffs further seek entry   of a permanent injunction prohibiting Defendant from any further infringing activities concerning the Copyrighted Recordings. Plaintiffs maintain Defendant's conduct is causing and, unless enjoined, will continue to cause, Plaintiffs great and irreparable injury such that monetary damages will be inadequate. As such, pursuant to 17 U.S.C. §§ 502 and 503, Plaintiffs seek injunctive relief prohibiting Defendant from further infringing Plaintiffs' copyrights, including any obtained on works later created, and ordering Defendant to destroy all copies of sound recordings made in violation of Plaintiffs' exclusive rights. (Compl. ¶ 16).
The Copyright Act permits a court to "grant temporary and final injunctions on such terms as it may deem reasonable to prevent or restrain infringement of a copyright." 17 U.S.C. § 502(a). A grant of injunctive relief requires the requesting party to establish: (1) a substantial likelihood of success on the merits; (2) a substantial threat that failure to grant the injunction will result in irreparable injury; (3) the threatened injury outweighs the threatened harm the injunction may cause the opposing party; and (4) the injunction will not disserve the public interest. Enrique Bernat F., S.A. v. Guadalajara, Inc., 210 F.3d 439, 442 (5th Cir. 2000).
In copyright cases, irreparable   harm is presumed on a showing of reasonable likelihood of success on the merits. Lexmark Int'l, Inc. v. Static Control Components, Inc., 387 F.3d 522, 532-33 (6th Cir. 2004); MyWebGrocer, LLC v. Hometown Info, Inc., 375 F.3d 190, 193 (2nd Cir. 2004); Elvis Presley Enters., Inc. v. Passport Video, 349 F.3d 622, 627 (9th Cir. 2003). Because the default against Defendant satisfies the element of success on the merits, Plaintiffs need not make any additional showing of irreparable harm. See NXIVM Corp. v. Ross Inst., 364 F.3d 471, 476 (2nd Cir. 2004) (to demonstrate likelihood of success on merits of copyright claim, plaintiff must establish ownership of valid copyright and unauthorized copying by defendant).
As to the third factor, Plaintiffs allege damages will not adequately compensate them for the harm caused. The Court agrees. Defendant's use of an online media distribution system with millions of potential users has made Plaintiffs' sound recordings vulnerable to further acts of infringement by countless others who can readily download and distribute the Copyrighted Recordings at issue. Further, there is no evidence Defendant has stopped infringing Plaintiffs' recordings, nor any evidence Defendant will stop, absent an injunction. Moreover, Defendant's failure to respond to Plaintiffs' complaint suggests Defendant does not take seriously the illegality of the infringing activity. Thus, without an injunction, Plaintiffs' copyrighted recordings would remain vulnerable to continued,   repeated infringement by Defendant. See Fermata Int'l Melodies, Inc. v. Champions Golf Club Inc., 712 F. Supp. 1257, 1262 (S.D. Tex. 1989), aff'd, 915 F.2d 1567 (5th Cir. 1990) (in short, an injunction is appropriate if liability is established and there is substantial likelihood of further infringement of plaintiffs' copyrights). Finally, injunctions are regularly issued pursuant to the mandate of section 502 because the "public interest is the interest in upholding copyright protections." Autoskill, Inc. v. Nat'l Educ. Support Sys., Inc., 994 F.2d 1476, 1499 (10th Cir. 1993). See Apple Computer, Inc. v. Franklin Computer Corp., 714 F.2d 1240, 1255 (3d Cir. 1983) (public interest can only be served by upholding copyright protections, thus preventing misappropriation of skills, creative energies, and resources invested in protected work). An injunction is necessary to preserve the integrity of the copyright laws which seek to encourage individual efforts and creativity by granting valuable enforceable rights. Atari Inc. v. Am. Philips Consumer Elec. Corp., 672 F.2d 607, 620 (7th Cir. 1982). An injunction will not harm the public interest, but rather, serve it.
All four factors necessary for the grant of injunctive relief are satisfied in this action. Because Defendant has knowingly infringed and shared Plaintiffs' copyrighted works, and no evidence has been presented showing Defendant has ceased the unauthorized activities, the injunction is proper in order to prevent further downloading and distribution of the copyrighted material.
Plaintiffs additionally argue, because   they continually create new copyrighted works similarly vulnerable to infringement, an injunction limited to existing works would require repetitive litigation to redress future infringements. They therefore request the injunction be addressed both to existing and future works. This Court agrees with Plaintiffs, and finds such an injunction is warranted. See, e.g., Princeton Univ. Press v. Mich. Document Serv., 99 F.3d 1381, 1392-93 (6th Cir. 1996) (weight of authority supports extension of injunctive relief to future works); Olan Mills, Inc. v. Linn Photo Co., 23 F.3d 1345, 1349 (9th Cir. 1994) (permanent injunction includes works created in the future). Accordingly, Plaintiffs are entitled to both present and future injunctive relief.
IT IS FURTHER ORDERED that as a sanction pursuant to Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 37(b)(2)(C) and 37 (d), Defendant Benjie Hal Blumenreich's Answer filed June 13, 2006 (Clerk's Dkt. #3) is hereby STRUCK and JUDGMENT OF DEFAULT is rendered in favor of the Plaintiffs the issue of Defendant Blumenreich's LIABILITY for the alleged violations of the Copyright Act as described in its Complaint and this Order.
IT IS FURTHER ORDERED that Plaintiffs have judgment against Defendant Benjie Hal Blumenreich in the amount of $750 per infringed work, as authorized under the Copyright Act (17 U.S.C. § 504(c)(1)), for each of the sound recordings listed in Exhibit A of the Complaint and Schedule 1, Appendix Sec. H. Accordingly, having been adjudged to be in default, Defendant shall pay damages to Plaintiffs for infringement of Plaintiffs' copyrights in the sound recordings listed in Exhibit A to the Complaint and Schedule 1, in the total principal sum of TWELVE THOUSAND DOLLARS ($12,000).
